  <!-- ========== 3. WHAT MOVED (Last 9 days, Jun 11-19) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Jun 11–19, 2026)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On 2026-06-11, Iranian officials held a working session with Qatari mediators in Tehran that narrowed the outstanding gaps on frozen asset release, Hormuz reopening protocols, and management of Iran's nuclear stockpile during a proposed 60-day ceasefire. The session was coordinated with the United States. On 2026-06-12, Pakistani Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif confirmed that the US and Iran had reached agreement on final MOU text. These two events — a confirmed, mediator-convened session and a named head-of-government confirmation — satisfied the YES resolution criteria for <span class="cite">F1</span> before its EOD Jun 16 horizon, and provided the evidentiary basis that also resolved <span class="cite">F2</span>. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>US Vice President JD Vance stated on 2026-06-15 that the MOU had been digitally signed by named US and Iranian representatives on 2026-06-14. On 2026-06-17, President Trump signed the document at a dinner at Versailles with French President Macron, and Iranian President Pezeshkian signed remotely from Tehran. The 14-point Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding commits both sides to an immediate halt to military operations on all fronts including Lebanon, directs the US to begin lifting its naval blockade within 30 days, and grants toll-free commercial passage through the Strait of Hormuz for 60 days. Nuclear program terms, ballistic missile issues, and the fate of proxy forces are explicitly deferred to the 60-day negotiation window. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>On 2026-06-14, IDF conducted air strikes in the Beirut area. Iran prepared a retaliatory response; a direct Trump-Netanyahu call and subsequent US diplomatic communication to Iranian principals caused Iran to stand down before executing a counter-strike. No TIER1 source confirmed a two-way direct Israel-Iran military exchange (Israeli strikes on Iranian territory AND Iranian strikes on Israeli territory within a 48h window) at any point between Jun 10 and Jun 19. The Jun 7–8 exchange predated the <span class="cite">F3</span> resolution window (opened Jun 10) and cannot retroactively satisfy criteria. The one-directional Jun 14 IDF action — without an executed Iranian counter — falls under the ambiguity rule ("one-directional strikes within the 48h window = AMBIGUOUS-RESOLVE-AS-NO"). The Islamabad MOU signed Jun 17 further reduces the probability of a qualifying two-way exchange in the remaining 5-day window (Jun 19–24); <span class="cite">F3</span> remains ACTIVE at 20%. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>AIS-tracked Hormuz transit data: on 2026-06-17, 26 vessels transited — 13 inbound, 13 outbound — the highest single-day total of the conflict, including three Iranian NITC supertankers that moved on Jun 16. As of Jun 18, outbound commercial maritime traffic remained in operational paralysis despite the MOU signing, with fewer than 10 independent commercial vessels transiting daily on a 7-day average. Kpler estimated a ramp to approximately 40 transits/day within 30 days if no MOU breakdown occurs. The 7-day average as of Jun 19 is materially below the 50/day threshold for <span class="cite">F4</span>, with 13 days remaining to that horizon — making the Jul 2 deadline for F4 a long-shot while keeping the Jul 19 horizon for <span class="cite">N1</span> genuinely contested. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>On 2026-06-19, the Burgenstock summit convened in Switzerland. Named US principal Steve Witkoff and Iranian FM Abbas Araghchi met face-to-face with Pakistan and Qatar as co-mediators present. This session is focused on MOU implementation details, not nuclear specifics. Araghchi stated publicly on Jun 17 that no nuclear talks would begin until MOU implementation commences — placing the start of nuclear-focused sessions in mid-July at earliest, per the US blockade-removal timeline of 30 days. The Knesset dissolution bill remained at first-reading stage with no second reading scheduled as of Jun 19; haredi parties backed away from an imminent push after Netanyahu offered to shelve the draft-exemption bill, reducing but not eliminating the pressure on the Jul 15 deadline for <span class="cite">F6</span> and the Jul 31 horizon for <span class="cite">N3</span>. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:15%;">Escalation 15%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:25%;">Protracted 25%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:60%;">De-esc 60%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> An MOU implementation failure — Israel resuming strikes on Iranian targets or Iran failing to allow Hormuz passage — collapses the 60-day window and re-activates direct military exchange dynamics (F3 analog).</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Nuclear and missile talks stall past the 60-day deadline, Hormuz reopens partially but P&amp;I cover is not reinstated, and the ceasefire holds in a frozen conflict without formal agreement — <span class="cite">N2</span> resolves NO, <span class="cite">N1</span> stays short of 50/day.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> The 60-day ceasefire holds, Hormuz transits ramp toward 40–50/day, nuclear talks begin by mid-July per <span class="cite">N2</span>, and the Islamabad MOU converts to a fuller framework — the dominant near-term path given both sides' signatures today.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Tasnim framed the MOU signing of Jun 14–17 as a vindication of Iran's "resistance" posture: the 14-point agreement is presented as Washington's acceptance of Iran's non-surrender terms, with emphasis on the $300 billion reconstruction fund and the US commitment to lift the naval blockade within 30 days. State media did not describe the signing as a concession; it ran under headlines emphasizing Iran's right to "safe passage" and the end of the US blockade — rather than the nuclear-supervision clauses. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>FM Araghchi's Jun 17 statement — "no nuclear talks until the MOU is implemented" — was carried prominently on IRNA and Press TV as a firm precondition, not a delay. The framing separates the ceasefire track from the nuclear track and constructs a sequenced narrative in which Iran controls the pace of the next phase. This is the same rhetorical structure Iran has used in prior negotiation cycles: acknowledge the deal, but hold the nuclear file as leverage for implementation compliance. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Kayhan, which reliably tracks hardliner opinion, ran commentary on Jun 18 describing the IDF Jun 14 Beirut strikes as a violation of the spirit of the MOU and calling on the government to hold the US responsible for Israeli action. No IRGC statement characterized the Burgenstock session as a capitulation. Press TV coverage of the Hormuz reopening emphasized that the 60-day toll-free window is Iran's offer — not a forced concession — and that Iran retains the right to close the Strait again if the terms are violated, a framing consistent with the MOU's ambiguity on enforcement. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

